.TH TMUX-AGENT-PICKER 1 "2026-11-16" "tmux-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tmux-agent-picker \- pick an agent pane anywhere on a tmux server and jump to it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tmux-agent-picker
.B \-\-socket
.I socket
.B \-\-client
.I client
.br
.B tmux-agent-picker
.B \-\-socket
.I socket
.B \-\-list
.RB [ \-\-client
.IR client ]
.br
.B tmux-agent-picker
.B \-\-socket
.I socket
.B \-\-preview
.I pane
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tmux-agent-picker
lists the panes of every session on the tmux server at
.I socket
that need attention or run an agent, in an
.BR fzf (1)
popup on
.IR client ,
most urgent first, with a preview of the pane under the cursor. Enter takes
the client to the chosen pane; Escape closes the popup and changes nothing
beyond the side effect of the popup described below.
.PP
Bind it with the server's socket and the client that pressed the key:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bind W run-shell 'tmux-agent-picker --socket #{q:socket_path} --client #{q:client_name}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each row shows a dot for its level, the pane's
.IB session : window . pane
location (24 columns, cut with an ellipsis), the config's
.B @pane_label
for the pane, and on agent rows the base name of the pane's directory. A
failed command adds
.BI exit " N"
after its red dot. A pane in a mode such as copy mode shows
.BR [COPY] ,
and the client's own pane shows
.BR here .
The location is only for reading: every target the command uses is an id.
.PP
Rows come in five levels, each pane once, at the first level that holds:
.TP
.B red
.B @pane_jump_failed
.TP
.B yellow
.B @pane_jump_waiting
.TP
.B green
.B @pane_jump_done
.TP
.B blue
.B @pane_jump_working
.TP
.B idle
.BR @pane_agent ,
dimmed, at the bottom
.PP
Within a level, rows follow the walk of sessions, windows and panes that
tmux's
.B #{S:#{W:#{P:...}}}
loops make, which is how a config's own
.B prefix a
style attention list goes, and a window linked into several sessions counts
for one of them only, as
.B @window_counted
decides. The levels and the order come from tmux formats alone: the command
never runs
.BR ps (1)
or reads
.IR /proc .
.PP
The preview shows the pane's visible screen with its colours, read from the
pane itself rather than a copy-mode view. Trailing lines that are blank once
their colour codes are removed are dropped, and the last lines that fit are
shown, so an agent's prompt at the bottom is in view. It reruns when the
cursor moves, and ctrl-r reloads the rows and the preview together.
.SS The jump
The popup only picks; the jump runs after it has closed. The command finds
where the pane lives now, so a pane moved to a new window, or a session
killed while a grouped session still holds the pane, is followed. A pane
that has closed is reported with a message and nothing moves. A pane in the
client's own session is selected there. Otherwise the client switches to a
session holding the pane, preferring one no other client is attached to, so
a grouped session another client is looking at keeps that client's view; when
every such session is attached, that client's view may change too.
.PP
Every command of the jump names the pane, and in the client's own session
the pane is made its window's active pane before the window is selected. No
other pane is made current on the way, so a config's hooks that acknowledge
the pane being visited never acknowledge a bystander. The chosen pane keeps
its mode.
.SS Side effect of the popup
From tmux 3.6, opening and closing a popup sends focus events to the pane
beneath it. A config that acknowledges attention on
.B pane-focus-in
or
.B pane-focus-out
therefore acknowledges the client's current pane when the picker opens, even
if the popup is cancelled, as for any popup. Other panes are unaffected.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The embedding tmux config supplies the predicates; the command evaluates
them with
.BR #{E:...} .
These are required:
.TP
.B @pane_jump_failed\fR, \fB@pane_jump_waiting\fR, \fB@pane_jump_done
The red, yellow and green classes. Each pane should match at most one, as
for a
.B prefix a
attention jump.
.TP
.B @pane_jump_working
Work in progress without attention: the blue class.
.TP
.B @pane_agent
The pane runs an agent: listed, dimmed, when nothing else holds.
.TP
.B @window_counted
The window counts for the session being walked. A window linked into
several sessions, as grouped sessions share theirs, should count for one of
them only; the example counts it for the first session it names, matched
exactly even when the name holds glob characters.
.PP
A required option the config leaves unset is an error that names it, so a
config written for an older picker is told what it lacks rather than shown
an empty list. Set one to
.B 0
to turn it off.
These are optional, and expand empty, which reads as false, when unset:
.TP
.B @pane_marked_failed
A local failed command; only then does a red row show the exit status from
.BR @term_notify_exit_status ,
and never for the
.B blocked
kind.
.TP
.B @pane_label
The row's label.
.TP
.B @pane_interactive
An interactive pane that is not an agent also shows its directory's base
name.
.PP
A pane is a candidate only while it has
.BR @term_notify_pending ,
.BR @termnav_child_attention ,
.B @term_busy
or
.BR @pane_agent ,
so most panes are skipped after plain option reads. A minimal example, the
predicates the tests run, for marks set by
.BR term-pane-mark (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
set -g @agent_names 'claude|codex|gemini|grok|muse|opencode'
set -g @pane_agent '#{m/r:^(#{@agent_names})$,#{pane_current_command}}'
set -g @pane_marked_failed '#{&&:#{@term_notify_pending},#{||:#{==:#{@term_notify_pending},blocked},#{>:#{@term_notify_exit_status},0}}}'
set -g @child_failed '#{m/r:^[0-9]+:[0-9]+:[0-9]+:[1-9][0-9]*$,#{@termnav_child_attention}}'
set -g @pane_jump_failed '#{||:#{E:@pane_marked_failed},#{E:@child_failed}}'
set -g @pane_jump_waiting '#{&&:#{!=:#{E:@pane_jump_failed},1},#{==:#{@term_notify_pending},waiting}}'
set -g @pane_jump_done '#{&&:#{!=:#{E:@pane_jump_failed},1},#{&&:#{!=:#{E:@pane_jump_waiting},1},#{@term_notify_pending}}}'
set -g @pane_jump_working '#{@term_busy}'
set -g @session_name_glob '#{s/([][*?\e\e])/\e\e\e1/:session_name}'
set -g @window_counted '#{||:#{==:#{window_linked_sessions_list},#{session_name}},#{m:#{E:@session_name_glob}#,*,#{window_linked_sessions_list}}}'
set -g @pane_label '#{pane_current_command}#{?#{!=:#{pane_title},#{host}}, · #{q/h:pane_title},}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B @termnav_child_attention
lease (expiry:working:pending:failed) is how termnav reports a nested tmux; a
real config also checks its expiry. cgraf78/dotfiles has a complete set,
with leases, sticky kinds and shell prompts taken into account.
.B @pane_label
may double
.B #
for tmux's renderer, as
.B q/h
does; the picker turns
.B ##
back into
.BR # .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " socket"
The tmux server's socket, as an absolute path, usually
.B #{q:socket_path}
from the binding. Required in every mode: an inherited
.B TMUX
can name another server, as over SSH or from a nested client.
.TP
.BI \-\-client " client"
The client to open the popup on and to move, usually
.B #{q:client_name}
from the binding. With
.BR \-\-list ,
it marks that client's pane
.BR here .
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the rows and exit. Each line holds the pane, window and session ids,
then the visible text, separated by tabs. ctrl-r runs this.
.TP
.BI \-\-preview " pane"
Print the preview of
.I pane
(a
.BI % N
id), or
.B (pane closed)
when it is gone. The popup's preview runs this, and reads
.B FZF_PREVIEW_LINES
(default 40).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A failure exits 1 and shows its reason on the client as a message as well
as on standard error, since
.B run-shell
discards a command's standard error. Failures are: a tmux query that fails
or answers the snapshot with something unexpected, a required option left
unset, a quote in a quoted path (below), a popup tmux could not open, an fzf
error, a chosen line that is not one of the rows, and a jump that fails while
the pane is still there. An fzf error (an option an older fzf does not know,
say) keeps the popup open on fzf's own message until Enter, since older fzf
draws its screen on standard error and the message cannot be captured.
.PP
A client that detaches while the popup is open, or a popup closed under fzf
(as by
.BR "display-popup -C" ),
is not a failure: the command exits 0 and says nothing. Neither does any
failure once the client has gone, since nobody is left to tell, and since
tmux 3.2a crashes its server when a key binding's blocking
.B run-shell
exits non-zero after its client has detached.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
tmux 3.2 or newer, and
.BR fzf (1)
0.27.0 or newer, the first to accept the
.B border-top
preview window option (checked by hand against 0.27.0 and 0.26.0; CI runs
the distributions' own fzf). tmux 3.2 cannot target a message at a client: it rejects
.B display-message -c
.I client
with its usage line, and only then does a message go to the session's most
recently active client instead. Before
tmux 3.6 a popup sends no focus events, so opening the picker acknowledges
nothing.
.PP
Without
.BR fzf (1)
on
.BR PATH ,
or with no rows, the command shows a tmux message saying so and exits
successfully. A quote in the socket path, the client name, the command's own
path or the temporary directory is refused, since they are quoted into the
popup's command line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A pane was chosen, or the popup was cancelled, matched nothing or closed
under fzf; or the client was gone, detached or in control mode, the pane had
closed, or a missing
.BR fzf (1)
or an empty list was reported through tmux.
.TP
.B 1
A failure, as under DIAGNOSTICS, or fzf returned a line that is not one of
the rows.
.TP
.B 64
The arguments were invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR fzf (1),
.BR tmux-command-palette (1),
.BR term-pane-mark (1),
.BR term-pane-state (1)
